---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import ConceptMap from "../components/ConceptMap.astro";
import Doodle from "../components/Doodle.astro";

const chapters = (await getCollection("chapters", ({ data }) => !data.draft)).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order,
);
const extras = await getCollection("extras", ({ data }) => !data.draft);
const first = chapters[0];
const total = chapters.reduce((n, c) => n + c.data.minutes, 0);

const PARTS = [
  { id: "orientation",   title: "Orientation",        art: "inversion",
    gist: "What this field actually is, where it sits next to normal programming, and a working model running in your browser before any theory." },
  { id: "foundations",   title: "How learning works", art: "hill",
    gist: "Loss, gradients, overfitting. The machinery is identical whether you're fitting a line or a language model, so we build it once, by hand." },
  { id: "deep-learning", title: "Deep learning",      art: "layers",
    gist: "Neural networks built from matrix multiplies, then backpropagation derived on paper, then the same thing in PyTorch in a tenth of the code." },
  { id: "frontier",      title: "The modern stack",   art: "beam",
    gist: "Convolutions, embeddings, attention, transformers — and how a next-token predictor became something that answers your questions." },
  { id: "practice",      title: "Doing it for real",  art: "ship",
    gist: "Reading papers, picking metrics, shipping a model, and knowing when the honest answer is that machine learning isn't the tool." },
];
---

<Base title="Gradient">
  <main class="home" id="main">
    <div class="hero">
      <div>
        <h1 class="home__title">Machine learning,<br />from the ground up.</h1>
        <p class="home__lede">
          Fourteen days. Every code block runs, right here in the page. Written
          for someone who already ships software and would like to know
          <em>where every piece fits</em> — not a survey, not a maths course, and
          not a tour of one library's API.
        </p>
        <div class="home__cta">
          {first && <a class="btn btn--primary" href={`/learn/${first.id}/`}>Start day one →</a>}
          <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/map/">See the whole map</a>
          <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/setup/">Set up your machine</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero__art"><Doodle name="pipWave" /></div>
    </div>

    <div class="pip-note">
      <Doodle name="pip" decorative />
      <p>
        This is Pip. Pip rolls downhill, which turns out to be the entire
        algorithm behind everything here — you'll meet the joke properly on day
        five.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>How this works</h2>
    <p>
      Press <strong>Run</strong> on any code block and Python starts inside your
      browser. No install, nothing to configure, works offline, works on a plane.
      The three chapters that need real PyTorch say so and connect to a kernel on
      your own machine instead.
    </p>
    <p>
      Python constructs are underlined and explained on hover — once, with the
      Rust construct you'd already reach for. Derivations are folded away, so the
      path reads end to end without opening a single one, and every one of them is
      there the moment you want it.
    </p>

    <h2>The path</h2>
    <div class="parts">
      {
        PARTS.map((part, i) => {
          const items = chapters.filter((c) => c.data.part === part.id);
          if (!items.length) return null;
          return (
            <div class="part">
              <div class="part__head">
                <span class="part__art"><Doodle name={part.art} decorative /></span>
                <h3>Part {["I", "II", "III", "IV", "V"][i]} · {part.title}</h3>
              </div>
              <p>{part.gist}</p>
              <ol>
                {items.map((c) => (
                  <li><a href={`/learn/${c.id}/`}>{c.data.title}</a></li>
                ))}
              </ol>
            </div>
          );
        })
      }
    </div>
    <p class="home__meta">
      {chapters.length} chapters · about {Math.round(total / 60)} hours of reading
      and running, spread over two weeks.
    </p>

    <h2>And when you want more</h2>
    <p>
      Keeping a promise about two weeks means leaving things out, and a lot of
      what got left out is lovely. It lives in
      <a href="/extras/">{extras.length} extras</a> — the deeper whys, the roads
      not taken, and the things you only learn by shipping. Nothing in the
      fourteen days depends on any of it, which is exactly what makes it safe to
      write.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it all fits</h2>
    <p>
      Before anything else, the shape of the field. Solid boxes are taught here;
      dashed ones exist and are deliberately out of scope. You won't understand
      most of it yet — that's the design. Come back after each part and watch it
      fill in.
    </p>
    <ConceptMap compact />
  </main>
</Base>
